window.docContents[189].resolve({"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Logical-Connectives-as-Inductive-Types--True--and--False":{"contents":"\n\nThe inductive definition of the True proposition is simple:inductive True : Prop where\n  | intro : True\nIt has one constructor: there is one definitional way to build True.\n(But there are lots of ways to state and prove true things!)Construct a proof object for the following proposition.theorem p_implies_true : ∀ (P : Prop), P → True :=\n  sorry\nFalse is equally simple — indeed, so simple it may look\nsyntactically wrong at first glance!inductive False : Prop\nThat is, False is an inductive type with no constructors —\ni.e., no way to build evidence for it. For example, there is\nno way to complete the following definition such that it\nsucceeds.theorem contra : False :=\n  True.intro\nBut it is possible to destruct False by pattern matching. There can\nbe no patterns that match it, since it has no constructors. To\nhandle this, Lean uses the nomatch construct.theorem false_implies_zero_eq_one : False → 0 = 1 :=\n  fun contra => nomatch contra\nSince there are no branches to evaluate, the nomatch expression\ncan be given any type we want, including 0 = 1.\nFortunately, it's impossible to ever cause the nomatch to be\nevaluated, because we can never construct a value of type False\nto pass to the function.Construct a proof object for the following proposition.theorem ex_falso_quodlibet' : ∀ (P : Prop), False → P :=\n  sorry\n\n\nend Props\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009ProofObjects: The Curry-Howard Correspondence\u0009Logical Connectives as Inductive Types","header":"10.6.4. True  and  False","id":"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Logical-Connectives-as-Inductive-Types--True--and--False"}});